data.content:
Since you have no DI and this is a small class library, the **lightest option** is an ambient-context `Clock` class using `AsyncLocal<T>`. No container needed, parallel-test-safe, and production cost is just one null check.

Here's the approach:

**1. Add a `Clock` class** — your single source of "now":

```csharp
// InvoiceLib/Clock.cs
namespace InvoiceLib;

public static class Clock
{
    private static readonly AsyncLocal<Func<DateTimeOffset>?> s_override = new();

    public static DateTimeOffset UtcNow
        => s_override.Value?.Invoke() ?? DateTimeOffset.UtcNow;

    /// <summary>Returns a scope that resets the override on dispose.</summary>
    public static IDisposable Override(DateTimeOffset fixedTime)
    {
        s_override.Value = () => fixedTime;
        return new Scope();
    }

    private sealed class Scope : IDisposable
    {
        public void Dispose() => s_override.Value = null;
    }
}
```

**2. Replace direct calls** in your classes:

```csharp
public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
    => Clock.UtcNow > dueDate;
```

**3. In tests**, pin the time:

```csharp
using (Clock.Override(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 15, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)))
{
    Assert.True(calculator.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 14, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
}
```

**Why this works well for a library with no DI:**

| Concern | Answer |
|---------|--------|
| Parallel tests | `AsyncLocal<T>` scopes per async flow — no interference |
| Production perf | One null-check per call (essentially free) |
| Setup cost | Zero config, zero packages |

If you later adopt DI or move to .NET 8+, you can swap the internals to delegate to `TimeProvider.System` — the call sites won't change.

